.TH PEKIT 1 2026-10-28 "Peios pekit 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
pekit \- build, test, run, and package GCSL build files as cached, sandboxed steps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pekit build
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR TARGET ...
.br
.B pekit test
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ TEST ...]
.br
.B pekit run
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I NAME
.RI [ ARG ...]
.br
.B pekit query deps
.RB [ \-\-kind
.IR KIND ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR TARGET ...
.br
.B pekit query rdeps
.RB [ \-\-kind
.IR KIND ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I FILE
.RI [ TARGET ...]
.br
.B pekit package
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ PACKAGE ...]
.br
.B pekit publish
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.B \-\-to
.I STORE
.IR PACKAGE ...
.br
.B pekit release
.RB [ \-j
.IR JOBS ]
.RB [ \-\-trace
.IR FILE ]
.B \-\-to
.I STORE
.I PACKAGE
.br
.B pekit lock
.RB [ \-\-update ]
.br
.B pekit clean
.RB [ \-\-cache ]
.br
.B pekit
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pekit
is a build system whose build files are GCSL.
It evaluates the definitions named on its command line in the workspace that holds the current directory, runs each call of a function marked
.B #[hermetic]
as a step in a sandbox of its own, and keeps every step's result in a store shared by every workspace on the machine.
A step whose code, arguments, input contents, platform, and the contexts it read are the same as a step's it recorded is found in the store and not run.
.PP
The workspace is the directory tree under the nearest
.I workspace.gcsl
at or above the current directory.
.BR build ,
.BR test ,
.BR run ,
.BR query ,
.BR package ,
and
.B publish
first put back each library
.I pekit.lock
pins that is missing from
.IR .gcsl/libraries/ ,
and warn of each one there that differs from its pin.
.PP
A target is a definition of the command's definition type: a
.B build
for
.BR build ,
a
.B test
for
.BR test ,
a
.B package
for
.BR package ,
.BR publish ,
and
.BR release ,
and a
.B script
or
.B build
for
.BR run .
A target is named by its name,
.BR hello ,
by its directory relative to the workspace's root and its name,
.BR app/hello ,
or, for one in the root directory,
.BR /hello .
A name alone means the one definition of that name, else the one in the current directory; if neither decides, the command fails and lists each candidate.
A glob, a name with
.B *
in a part, selects every definition it matches, relative to the current directory: its last part is the name and the parts before it are directories.
In a part,
.B *
matches any run of characters;
.B **
matches any number of directories, none included, and as the last part every name in them.
.B .
is every definition of the current directory.
A glob starting with
.B /
is relative to the workspace's root.
A definition selected more than once is built once.
.PP
Several targets are one build: a step two of them reach runs once.
A target that fails does not stop the others.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B build
Build each
.IR TARGET .
For each, print the files it gave, as paths relative to the current directory under
.IR pekit-out/ ,
and the number of steps run and found in the store; or its error.
.TP
.B test
Run each
.IR TEST ,
or every
.B test
definition in the current directory and below it if none is given.
Print each test's result,
.BR passed ,
.B passed (cached)
when its step was found in the store,
.B flaky
when it passed only on a retry, or
.B FAILED
followed by its failure's message; then the number that passed and failed.
.TP
.B run
Run the
.B script
.IR NAME ,
or build the
.B build
.I NAME
and run the one executable file it gives.
The words
.I ARG
are a script's
.BR ^script_session.args ,
or the program's arguments.
Options of
.B pekit
come before
.IR NAME ;
everything after it is
.IR ARG .
A program runs in the current directory, with the terminal, and
.B pekit
exits with its status.
Every output of the build is written to
.I pekit-out/
before a script's or the program's first command runs.
.B \-\-check\-reproducible
is refused.
.TP
.B query deps
Evaluate each
.I TARGET
without running a step, and print each step it reaches, with the workspace files the target's code and each step read, and the steps each step is given.
A target that waits for a step's result stops there, and the place it waits is printed.
.TP
.B query rdeps
Evaluate each
.IR TARGET ,
or every target of its kind in the current directory and below it if none is given, without running a step, and print each that reads
.IR FILE ,
a path relative to the current directory, one to a line.
A GCSL file is read by every target whose code is in its directory or below it.
A target that waits for a step's result is printed too, with a note on standard error.
.TP
.B package
Pack each
.IR PACKAGE ,
or every
.B package
definition in the current directory and below it if none is given, with each packager the workspace lists in
.BR pekitconf.packagers ,
and print where each was packed.
.TP
.B publish
Pack each
.I PACKAGE
as
.B package
does, then publish it with each packager into
.IR STORE ,
in program order, outside any step.
.B \-\-check\-reproducible
is refused.
.TP
.B release
Release
.IR PACKAGE :
check that the workspace is in a git repository and matches its
.BR HEAD ,
every file committed but
.I pekit-out/
and
.IR .gcsl/ ;
export the workspace's tree at
.B HEAD
into a directory of its own; build the package there as
.B \-\-strict \-\-check\-reproducible \-\-profile release
would; publish it into
.IR STORE ;
and tag
.B HEAD
.IR NAME @ VERSION
with a lightweight tag.
A tag of that name on another commit fails the release before anything is published.
A tag of that name on
.B HEAD
publishes again and adds no tag.
.TP
.B lock
Pin each library the workspace imports, and each library those import, that
.I pekit.lock
does not pin, at the commit its source's revision names, and export it into
.IR .gcsl/libraries/ .
Drop each pinned library that nothing imports, with its directory.
Print a line for each library pinned or dropped.
.TP
.B clean
Remove
.IR pekit-out/ .
.SH OPTIONS
.B build ,
.BR test ,
.BR run ,
.BR query ,
.BR package ,
and
.B publish
take these options.
.B release
takes
.B \-\-jobs
and
.B \-\-trace
only.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-reproducible
Build, then run every step again, reusing nothing from the store, and fail if a step given the same inputs both times gave other outputs.
Each such step is named, with each of its files that differed.
The second run records nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-gcsl\-profile " FILE"
Write a profile of the build's GCSL evaluation to
.IR FILE :
how many times the targets' and the steps' code ran and for how long, and for each function and built-in, its own time, its total time, its calls, and how many found a memoized result.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs " \fIJOBS\fR"
Run at most
.I JOBS
programs at once.
The default is the number of processors.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dynamic
Fail a target at the point it waits for a step's result.
.TP
.BI \-\-platform " PLATFORM"
Build for
.IR PLATFORM ,
written
.IR ARCH \- OS ,
such as
.BR aarch64\-linux ,
or
.IR ARCH ,
which keeps the operating system of the machine
.B pekit
runs on.
It sets the
.B platform
context for every target, where the workspace declares it.
Steps still run on the machine
.B pekit
runs on.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " PROFILE"
Use the keychain's secrets of
.IR PROFILE .
The default is
.BR dev .
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
Run every step and every program, reusing nothing from the store, and record each.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Build as a release does.
Steps are given none of the host's tools.
A target's own code may fetch, unpack, ask the keychain, and read paths under the workspace and the build's outputs; any other effect fails where the target makes it.
The build fails unless every library it imports is pinned in
.IR pekit.lock ,
none differs from its pin, and
.B GCSL_LIBRARY_PATH
is unset.
Steps marked
.B #[scratch]
are given an empty directory.
Steps are keyed apart from a build that is not strict, and not by the host's packages.
.TP
.BI \-\-trace " FILE"
Write a trace of the build to
.IR FILE ,
in the Trace Event Format: a span for each attempt of each step, each program, each staging and keeping of a step's files, and each run of a target's or a step's code.
The trace names programs but not their arguments.
.TP
.BI \-\-kind " KIND"
For
.B query
only: the definition type of the targets.
The default is
.BR build .
.TP
.BI \-\-to " STORE"
For
.B publish
and
.B release
only: the store to publish into, a path or a URL, as each packager reads it.
.TP
.B \-\-update
For
.B lock
only: pin every library again at its source's revision, not only those not pinned yet.
.TP
.B \-\-cache
For
.B clean
only: also remove the store, its scratch directories, and the directories steps are staged in, so that every step runs again.
The state directory is never removed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help, for
.B pekit
or for the command it follows.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every target was built, every test passed, the script or command succeeded, or help was printed.
For
.B run
of a program, the program's own status.
.TP
.B 1
A target failed to build, a test failed, the workspace failed to load or check, or the command failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was not understood.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PEKIT_CACHE_DIR
The cache directory, which holds the store, the git repositories of libraries, and the directories steps are staged in.
If it is unset or empty, the cache directory is
.I pekit
under
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME ,
else
.IR ~/.cache/pekit .
.TP
.B PEKIT_CACHE_LIMIT
The size the store is kept within, a number of bytes followed by nothing,
.BR B ,
.B K
or
.BR KiB ,
.B M
or
.BR MiB ,
.B G
or
.BR GiB ,
.B T
or
.BR TiB
in powers of 1024, or
.BR KB ,
.BR MB ,
.BR GB ,
or
.B TB
in powers of 1000.
.B 0
is no limit.
The default is 50 GiB.
When a build ends with the store larger, and no other
.B pekit
process has the store open, the store is evicted to nine tenths of the limit, cheapest to make again for its size first.
A value that is no size fails every command that builds.
.TP
.B PEKIT_STATE_DIR
The state directory, which holds the machine's key that secrets' identities are hashed with.
If it is unset or empty, the state directory is
.I pekit
under
.BR XDG_STATE_HOME ,
else
.IR ~/.local/state/pekit .
.TP
.B PEKIT_NO_SYNC
If set,
.B pekit
does not flush a file it puts into the store to the disk before it renames the file into place.
A crash can then leave a file in the store whose contents do not match its name.
.TP
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " HOME
Where the cache and state directories are, as
.B PEKIT_CACHE_DIR
and
.B PEKIT_STATE_DIR
describe.
.TP
.B GCSL_LIBRARY_PATH
Directories, separated by colons, searched for a library before
.IR .gcsl/libraries/ .
A strict build fails if it is set.
.TP
.BI GCSL_SECRET_FILE_ NAME
The file of the secret
.I NAME
for a profile the keychain has no entry for, the name in capitals with
.B _
for each character that is not a letter or a digit.
.TP
.BI GCSL_SECRET_ NAME
The value of the secret string
.I NAME
for a profile the keychain has no entry for, named as for
.BR GCSL_SECRET_FILE_ .
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.BR bwrap ,
which runs every step and every program of a target, and
.BR git ,
which fetches libraries and commits, are found.
Steps are not given it: a step's program whose
.B exec
sets no
.B PATH
runs with
.BR /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I workspace.gcsl
The workspace's root file.
Its
.B pekitconf
record sets the
.BR toolchains ,
.BR keychain ,
and
.B packagers
contexts for every target, where the workspace imports the module that declares each.
.TP
.I pekit.lock
The libraries the workspace pins, and where they come from; see
.BR pekit.lock (5).
.TP
.I .gcsl/libraries/
The pinned libraries, exported from their repositories.
.TP
.I pekit-out/
At the workspace's root: each step's outputs under
.IR .steps/FUNCTION/IDENTITY/ ,
each fetched file under
.IR .fetch/ ,
each unpacked archive under
.IR .unpack/ ,
each git commit's files under
.IR .git/ ,
and each target's results linked under the target's name and the step that made them.
.TP
.I CACHE/store/
The store: files by their SHA-256 digests in
.IR cas/ ,
step and program records in
.IR records/ ,
materialized trees in
.IR trees/ ,
the scratch directories of steps marked
.B #[scratch]
in
.IR scratch/ ,
bare clones of repositories
.B fetch_git
fetched in
.IR git/ ,
and what eviction keeps in
.IR usage/ ,
.IR size ,
and
.IR eviction.json .
Every process holds
.I lock
shared while it has the store open.
.TP
.I CACHE/git/
Bare clones of the repositories libraries are pinned from.
.TP
.I CACHE/scratch/
The directories steps are staged in, removed as each step ends.
.TP
.I STATE/keychain-key
The machine's key, readable only by its owner, made the first time it is needed.
.SH NOTES
Do not change the files in the store, or files under
.I pekit-out/.steps/
that are links to them: a file there is the store's own file, and a changed one reaches every later step given it.
The files a target gives are copies, which can be changed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pekit build hello
Build the definition
.B build hello
and print where its files are.
.TP
.B pekit build \-\-platform aarch64 \(aqapp/**\(aq
Build every
.B build
definition in
.I app
and below it for aarch64 Linux.
.TP
.B pekit test $(pekit query rdeps \-\-kind test app/main.c)
Run the tests that read
.IR app/main.c ,
and every test if the list is empty.
.TP
.B pekit run deploy \-\-dry\-run
Run the script
.B deploy
with the argument
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.TP
.B pekit release dev.example.greet \-\-to /srv/artifacts
Release the package
.B dev.example.greet
into
.IR /srv/artifacts ,
and tag the commit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bwrap (1),
.BR gcsl (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR gcsl (5),
.BR pekit.lock (5)
.PP
https://learn.peios.org/pekit
